// In-depth battles, batch p: cusco, tucapel, panipat2, okehazama, malta, talikota.
// Checked against the English, Portuguese, Spanish and Japanese Wikipedia articles on each battle and its leaders
// (Siege of Cusco, Manco Inca, Sacsayhuaman, Tucapel, Lautaro, Valdivia, Second Panipat, Hemu, Akbar, Bairam Khan,
// Okehazama, Imagawa Yoshimoto, Oda Nobunaga, Great Siege of Malta, Talikota, Rama Raya, Vijayanagara, Hampi) and the works
// they cite (Hemming, Quilter, Kubler, Flickema, Vivar, Gongora Marmolejo, Mariño de Lobera, Sarkar, Abu'l-Fazl, Qandhari,
// Fujimoto, Balbi, Bosio, Bradford, Sire, Eaton, Kamath, Kulke and Rothermund), plus a HistoryNet article (MacQuarrie),
// The Week (Pillai) and the Haryana tourism site. Britannica, World History Encyclopedia and The Diplomat returned errors
// and were not used.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "conquistador": "A Spanish soldier or adventurer who conquered lands in the Americas in the 16th century, usually on his own account and paying his own expenses in return for a promise of gold, land and titles.",
  "funda": "A strip of leather or cloth with which a stone is hurled by whirling it through the air. In the hands of a good slinger, a sling stone breaks a bone or dents a helmet.",
  "sapa inca": "The title of the Inca emperor, called the 'only Inca'. He was held to be a descendant of the Sun.",
  "boleadeiras": "Stones or metal balls tied to cords, thrown to wrap around the legs of a horse or a person and bring them down.",
  "orejón": "The name the Spaniards gave to the Inca nobles, because of the large earrings that stretched their earlobes ('orejas', in Spanish).",
  "toqui": "A war chief of the Mapuche, chosen to lead the people in times of war. In 1553, the toqui was Caupolicán, and Lautaro was his deputy.",
  "yanaconas": "Indigenous people who served the Spaniards, as porters, servants and auxiliary soldiers. Many came from Peru, others were from Chile.",
  "mogol": "From the Mughal Empire, the dynasty of Turkic and Mongol origin that ruled most of northern India from 1526 to 1857. Akbar was its third emperor.",
  "regente": "One who rules in the name of a king who is still a child or too young, or who lacks the strength to rule.",
  "ghazi": "A Muslim honorific title given to one who kills an enemy of the faith in combat. Bairam Khan wanted Akbar to receive it.",
  "howdah": "A cabin or platform on the back of a war elephant, from which the commander watched the battle and gave orders, and where archers and shooters sometimes rode.",
  "dinastia sur": "The Afghan dynasty of Sher Shah, which defeated Humayun in 1540 and forced him into exile. Humayun only retook Delhi in 1555. Hemu served one of the last Sur kings.",
  "daimiô": "A great feudal lord of Japan, owner of lands and head of an army of samurai. In the time of Okehazama, there were dozens of them, almost always at war with one another.",
  "sengoku": "The 'Warring States period' in Japan, from the mid-15th century to the early 17th, in which feudal lords fought one another and the shogun hardly ruled at all.",
  "palanquim": "A chair or cabin, carried on the shoulders of men, used to transport important people.",
  "grão-mestre": "The supreme head of a religious military order, such as that of the Hospitallers. He was elected by the knights and commanded all of them.",
  "sipahis": "Cavalrymen of the Ottoman army, who received land from the sultan in exchange for fighting on horseback. They were the heavy cavalry of the regular troops.",
  "corsário": "A ship captain who attacks and plunders enemy ships with a government's license. In the Mediterranean, there were Christian and Muslim corsairs, and both sides feared them.",
  "paxá": "The title of a high officer or governor of the Ottoman Empire: general, admiral or grand vizier.",
  "galé": "A long, low warship driven by oars, used in the Mediterranean. The rowers were generally slaves or prisoners.",
  "ravelim": "A small defensive work shaped like an arrowhead, built in front of a fort's main wall, to protect the entrance and provide a forward firing post.",
  "decão": "The great plateau of south-central India. In 1565, it was divided into Muslim sultanates, north of the Krishna river.",
  "sultanato": "A kingdom ruled by a sultan, a Muslim sovereign. Those of the Deccan were born from the breakup of the Bahmani Sultanate.",
  "bahmânidas": "The Bahmani Sultanate, a Muslim kingdom of the Deccan that broke up into smaller states, the sultanates of Ahmadnagar, Bijapur, Golconda, Bidar and Berar, which began to fight one another and Vijayanagara."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  cusco: {
    war: "Manco Inca's rebellion against the Spanish conquest of Peru (1536–1537)",
    hook: "About 190 Spaniards, locked in a burning city, against an army that the sources count in the tens of thousands.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "On 16 November 1532, at Cajamarca, the [[conquistador|conquistador]] Francisco Pizarro and his men captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa. The ransom was enormous, more than 18 tons of gold and silver, according to historians. The Spaniards took the ransom and, in July 1533, killed Atahualpa anyway.",
        "The empire, already emerging from a war between brothers, was left without leadership. Pizarro placed on the throne first Túpac Huallpa and, after his death, Manco Inca, son of the emperor Huayna Cápac. The Spaniards took Cusco, the capital, on 15 November 1533, after defeating the army of the general Quisquis, and Manco was crowned the following month.",
        "The treatment changed fast. When Francisco Pizarro left the city, his brothers Juan and Gonzalo were left in command, and they humiliated Manco, demanded gold and, according to Spanish Wikipedia, his wife and sister Cura Ocllo. After an attempt to escape, in November 1535, Juan imprisoned him. In January 1536, Hernando Pizarro, another brother, came back from Spain and released him.",
        { h: "The road to the siege" },
        { timeline: [
          ["November 1532", "Pizarro captures Atahualpa at Cajamarca."],
          ["15 November 1533", "The Spaniards take Cusco. Manco Inca is crowned the following month."],
          ["November 1535", "Manco tries to flee and is imprisoned by Juan Pizarro."],
          ["January 1536", "Hernando Pizarro releases him."],
          ["18 April 1536", "Manco leaves Cusco on the pretext of fetching a golden statue and performing ceremonies in the Yucay valley. He goes to Lares and plans the revolt with his chiefs."],
          ["May 1536", "The Incas besiege Cusco."]
        ]},
        "Manco's departure was a trap, and Hernando only realized it afterward. The Spaniards were divided: Diego de Almagro, Pizarro's older partner, was far away, on an expedition to Chile. Manco took advantage."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Manco Inca, about 20 years old" },
        "He had been born in Cusco around 1515, son of Huayna Cápac and half-brother of the emperor Huáscar. He fled Cusco when Atahualpa's generals took the city and, on 14 November 1533, met Pizarro. He was put on the throne by the Spaniards, but without real power. After his escape, he wanted to expel the Spaniards once and for all, and his plan was to defeat the troops in Cusco and also take Lima, Pizarro's new city. Not all the Incas followed him: part of the nobility, including his half-brother Paullu, stayed on the Spanish side.",
        { h: "Hernando Pizarro" },
        "The brother who commanded the city. He had released Manco believing his promises of gold, and admitted afterward that he had been deceived. He commanded the defense from inside Cusco during the siege.",
        { h: "Juan Pizarro, 23 years old" },
        "The youngest of the brothers present, head of the cavalry. It was he who led the attack on Sacsayhuamán, and he died of it.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Inca side, the general Paucar Huaman and the high priest Villac Umu (also written Willaq Umu) led the warriors at Sacsayhuamán. Titu Cusi Gualpa, an Inca noble, took command there when the two left. And there was a noble whose real name was lost: the Spaniards called him [[orejón|orejón]], and history kept the nickname Cahuide."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Spaniards in Cusco numbered about 190, some 80 of them on horseback, and a few thousand indigenous allies, according to John Hemming, the most cited historian on the subject. Another author, Kim MacQuarrie, counts 196 Spaniards, 110 of them on foot, plus African slaves and some 500 Cañari and Chachapoya allies. These were peoples who had been conquered by the Incas.",
        "The Inca army is the most uncertain point. Hemming speaks of 100,000 to 200,000 men, and other sources go down to 40,000. Most were farmers and herders called to war, not professional soldiers. Spanish Wikipedia says the army was divided into four corps, one for each region of the empire.",
        { note: "The numbers for both sides vary greatly from book to book. What can be said with certainty is that the Spaniards were a tiny minority in a sea of warriors and allies." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Cusco lies in a high valley of the Andes. Above the city, on a hill, stood Sacsayhuamán, an Inca fortress with three terraced walls, about 400 meters long and 6 high, made of giant stones that fit together without mortar. Pedro Pizarro, who saw it, described two very tall round towers inside. Whoever held Sacsayhuamán dominated the city, and the Spanish horses had great difficulty on the steep climb.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, in early May 1536, Juan Pizarro set out with some 70 horsemen to disperse the Incas gathered in the Yucay valley. He cleared the valley and held it for three or four days, until a messenger from Hernando arrived with the news that warriors were covering the hills around Cusco. Juan hurried back.",
        "Second, on 5 May the Incas occupied Sacsayhuamán, which the Spaniards had abandoned in order to concentrate on the city. Third, on 6 May the warriors attacked the central plaza. They hurled heated sling stones wrapped in cotton and fire arrows, which set the thatched roofs alight. The fire spread, and the chronicler Cristóbal de Molina wrote that the smoke was so thick that the Spaniards nearly suffocated. According to MacQuarrie, the building Hatun Cancha did catch fire, and some say African slaves put it out from the roof.",
        "Fourth, the Spaniards entrenched themselves in two large halls on the east side of the plaza, Suntur Huasi and Hatun Cancha, and from there made short sorties. The Incas learned to fight the horses: they pulled down walls on top of the horsemen, dug holes in the terraces, flooded the approaches and lassoed the animals' legs with [[bolas|boleadeiras]].",
        "Fifth, about a week later, the Spaniards decided to retake Sacsayhuamán, which was the Incas' base. Juan Pizarro set out with some 50 horsemen and allies, broke through the lines and pretended to flee toward Lima, which drew warriors out of the fortress. Then he circled back and attacked from outside the city.",
        "Sixth, in the assault on the walls, a stone struck Juan Pizarro on the head. He died days later. The next day, the Spaniards and their allies repelled the Inca counterattacks and, at night, took the terraces with ladders. The defenders lacked water and ammunition, and withdrew into the two towers.",
        "Seventh, Paucar Huaman and Villac Umu went out to fetch reinforcements at Calca, Manco's base, and left Titu Cusi Gualpa in command. When they returned, the fortress was already in Spanish hands. Many defenders threw themselves from the walls.",
        "Eighth, the fighting became a series of daily clashes, with a pause at every new moon, which the Incas observed for religious reasons. The Spaniards began to plunder the region for food and, according to Hemming, had all captured women killed and the men's hands cut off. Hernando went with about 100 Spaniards and some 30,000 allies to attack Ollantaytambo, where Manco had set up his base, and was defeated in January 1537: the Incas diverted the Patacancha river and flooded the ground, which neutralized the cavalry. Soon after, Manco prepared a new attack on Cusco. A Spanish patrol stumbled upon the Inca army by chance, the surprise was lost, and that night the Spaniards attacked first and caused many casualties.",
        { h: "The end of Cahuide" },
        "The best-known account is Pedro Pizarro's. An Inca noble, the 'orejón' whom history called Cahuide, defended one of the towers with a copper-tipped mace and with armor and a shield taken from the Spaniards. Hernando Pizarro ordered that he be taken alive. When the tower was about to fall, Cahuide wrapped himself in his cloak and threw himself from the top, so as not to surrender."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "English Wikipedia gives the siege as a period of ten months, and Manco lifted it. Both sides lost many people, but there are only estimates, and vague ones: MacQuarrie speaks of 2,000 to 4,000 Incas dead and some 35 Spaniards, and does not make clear which part of the fighting this refers to. What weighed most was hunger: the Inca army had left the fields to fight, the harvests were in danger, and Manco sent most of the men home to plant. According to Spanish Wikipedia, he suspended the siege on 18 April 1537. The end date varies from source to source: others point to early March.",
        "The decision has defenders and critics. For some historians, Manco threw away his only chance of expelling the Spaniards. For others, it was the only realistic way out, since Spanish reinforcements, coming from Chile with Almagro and from Lima with Alonso de Alvarado, were arriving. According to English Wikipedia, the Incas also defeated four relief expeditions coming from Lima, killing nearly 500 Spaniards, but that was not enough.",
        "Almagro returned to Cusco on 8 May 1537, took control and imprisoned Hernando and Gonzalo Pizarro for a few weeks. Gonzalo escaped. The dispute between Pizarristas and Almagristas became the first civil war among the conquistadors and ended at Las Salinas, in 1538, with Almagro's defeat.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the Incas' greatest attempt to expel the Spaniards. It failed, and the empire never again had the chance to rise as a whole. Manco withdrew first to Ollantaytambo and then to the mountains of Vilcabamba, where he founded a smaller Inca state, which resisted until 1572, when the last emperor, Túpac Amaru, was executed. Manco himself was murdered by the fugitive Almagristas he had sheltered, in 1542 or 1544, depending on the source.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Cahuide: the name is not certain. Wikipedia says his real name is unknown, and that the word may come from Quechua 'kawiri', 'lookout'. The scene of the leap comes from Spanish chroniclers, later repeated by popular and tourist sources; it works as a symbol, but the details are not proven." },
        { note: "The dates: the siege began on 6 May 1536 (some count 3 May) and ended between March and April 1537. When 'ten months' is said, it all falls within the same interval, but the exact date of each beginning and end is debated." },
        { note: "What is left of Sacsayhuamán: after the conquest, the Spaniards dismantled a good part of the complex to use the stone in buildings. The largest stones that remain are calculated, in modern estimates, at 128 to 200 tons." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  tucapel: {
    war: "Arauco War, in the Spanish conquest of Chile (1536 onward)",
    hook: "A former stable boy of Valdivia used what he learned from the Spanish horses to defeat his own master.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1540, Pedro de Valdivia left Cusco with about 150 Spaniards and 1,000 Peruvian auxiliaries to conquer Chile. He crossed the Atacama desert and founded Santiago on 12 February 1541. In 1550 he founded Concepción, after a hard fight against the Mapuche at the battle of Penco, and in the following years he planted cities farther south: La Imperial, Valdivia, Angol and Villarrica.",
        "The Mapuche, the people who lived in south-central Chile, had no king, but chose war chiefs in times of conflict. Valdivia spread his few soldiers among small forts, such as Tucapel and Purén, to dominate the territory and force the indigenous people to work in the gold mines. The further he advanced, the more the Mapuche resisted.",
        { h: "The road to Tucapel" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1540–1541", "Valdivia arrives in Chile and founds Santiago."],
          ["1550", "Founds Concepción."],
          ["1551–1552", "Founds cities and forts farther south."],
          ["1553", "The Mapuche decide to go to war. Caupolicán is the toqui, and Lautaro his deputy."],
          ["December 1553", "Lautaro destroys the fort of Tucapel. Valdivia leaves Concepción to relieve it."],
          ["25 December 1553", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "In the middle of this story was a young man. Captured as a boy by the Spaniards, Lautaro became Valdivia's stable boy. He looked after the horses and accompanied the troop in battle. The sources do not agree on the date of his escape, between 1550 and 1552. What matters is what he took with him: he knew how to ride, understood how the Spaniards fought, and had realized that the horse was an animal, not a monster."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Lautaro, about 19 years old" },
        "Born around 1534, son of a Mapuche chief, he was captured at age 11, according to Wikipedia. He learned to ride and, according to the same source, cavalry tactics from Captain Marcos Veas. He returned to his people and was elected vice-[[toqui|toqui]] by Caupolicán. His achievement was to combine what he had seen among the Spaniards with what the Mapuche already knew. He died young, on 29 April 1557, in a dawn ambush at Mataquito, aged 22 or 23.",
        { h: "Pedro de Valdivia, 56 years old" },
        "An Extremaduran soldier, born around 1497 (some sources give as late as 1505), he fought in Italy and then in Peru alongside Francisco Pizarro. He was sent by Pizarro as a lieutenant to the southern lands and became governor of Chile. He preferred farming and cattle raising to mining, but when rumors of gold appeared, he began to force the indigenous people to labor. In 1553 he had a lot of land to defend and few soldiers.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Spanish side, Captain Gómez de Almagro was at Purén, where Lautaro managed to pin him down and keep him from joining Valdivia. Luis de Bobadilla led the small patrol that went ahead, and the priest Bartolomé del Pozo accompanied Valdivia. On the Mapuche side, Caupolicán, the toqui, commanded the Mapuche in general, and it was he, according to the chronicler Jerónimo de Vivar, who ordered Valdivia executed."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Spaniards were few: modern estimates give 37 to 55, probably about 50. With them went between 2,000 and 5,000 [[yanaconas|yanaconas]], depending on the source. The Mapuche are hard to count. The chronicles, such as those of Vivar, Góngora Marmolejo and Mariño de Lobera, speak of 13,000 to 50,000, which historians find exaggerated. Modern estimates fall between 5,000 and 10,000, probably about 6,000.",
        { note: "Almost everything we know comes from Spanish chroniclers, who wrote about a defeat of their own. The Mapuche numbers remain in the dark, and so do Valdivia's: English Wikipedia gives 55 Spaniards at one point and 50 at another." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The fort of Tucapel stood near present-day Cañete, in the Araucanía region, in hilly terrain of forest and marsh. The horses ran well on flat ground and badly in mud. That detail weighed in the end.",
        { h: "The march" },
        "Valdivia left Concepción in mid-December 1553, passed through Quilacoya and gained more soldiers on the way. Mapuche spies followed the column without attacking. Puzzled by the lack of news and of attacks, he sent a patrol of five men, under Luis de Bobadilla, and camped half a day from the fort. He reached the fort on the morning of 25 December and found it destroyed: Lautaro had taken it with about 6,000 warriors, and the garrison had withdrawn to Purén. A request for reinforcements from Valdivia to Purén was intercepted.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Spaniards reached the ruined fort and began to set up camp. Second, Mapuche warriors came out of the forest and attacked. Valdivia formed three squadrons of about 20 men, and the first charge was repelled. Third, the Mapuche fell back up a steep slope, and the Spanish cavalry still attacked those who were withdrawing, but met spears, and turned back.",
        "Fourth, a second squadron, with spearmen and men armed with maces, ropes and lassos, faced the horsemen and pulled many out of their saddles to drag them away. The Spaniards repelled it too, but with losses. According to Spanish Wikipedia, a horn marked the time for each squadron to withdraw, to be replaced. Fifth, the third squadron arrived, led by Lautaro. The Spaniards had already lost half the troop, and the auxiliaries were dwindling.",
        "Sixth, Valdivia threw in everything that was left. Seeing the defeat, he ordered a retreat, but Lautaro attacked on the [[flank|flanco]]. Seventh, the Spanish line broke up and the Mapuche pursued the stragglers. Only Valdivia and the priest Pozo escaped, on the best horses. Eighth, the horses bogged down in the marshes near the Pilmaiquén and Pichidenquehue rivers, and the two were captured.",
        { h: "How Valdivia died" },
        "The sources tell it in different ways. For Vivar, Caupolicán asked for the execution, and Valdivia was killed with a spear, his head put on display. For Góngora Marmolejo, he offered to leave the Mapuche lands and hand over the cattle in exchange for his life, was refused, and after having his forearms cut off and eaten he was killed. For Mariño de Lobera, the offer was the same, and a warrior killed him with a mace for not trusting his word. Lobera also records a story of molten gold poured down Valdivia's throat, which is legend."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "All the Spaniards died and many of the auxiliaries as well. On the Mapuche side, Spanish Wikipedia speaks of more than 2,000 dead, without clear documentary support, and the English one says only that there were many. Among the dead were chiefs.",
        "It was the end of the myth of Spanish invincibility in open country. Caupolicán besieged the city of Valdivia and the settlements of the south, and Lautaro kept an eye on Concepción. In February 1554, with about 8,000 men, Lautaro defeated the Spaniard Francisco de Villagra at Marihueñu. In 1555, he took and destroyed Concepción. In 1556 he marched north, toward Santiago, but turned back after the fight at Peteroa. In April 1557, he died at Mataquito. His head was put on display in the plaza of Santiago.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Lautaro showed that Spanish weaponry was not invincible: the horse tired, bogged down and could be lassoed. The Mapuche learned to combine weapons, use ambushes and choose the terrain, and came to be seen as a serious adversary. Chile today considers him its first great general. The Mapuche resisted the Spaniards, and later the Chilean state, for more than three centuries.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The dates: Spanish Wikipedia says the battle may have been on 25 December 1553 or on 1 January 1554. Christmas Day is the most used date." },
        { note: "Valdivia's death: the story that Lautaro tortured him for three days and that the chiefs ate his heart is a later legend. Spanish Wikipedia finds a quick death, like a warrior's, more likely. The same source says the skull was turned into a chicha cup among the chiefs." },
        { note: "The former stable boy: Isabel Allende's novel says that Lautaro let himself be captured on purpose. It is fiction, not history." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  panipat2: {
    war: "Reconquest of India by the Mughals under Akbar (1555–1556)",
    hook: "A saltpeter seller who became a king was winning the battle when a stray arrow struck him in the eye.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The [[Mughal|mogol]] Empire was founded in 1526 by Babur, who took northern India after defeating the sultan of Delhi at the first battle of Panipat. His son Humayun lost the throne in 1540 to Sher Shah, of the [[Sur dynasty|dinastia sur]], and spent 15 years in exile. He only recovered Delhi in 1555, and died in January 1556 (27 January, according to Wikipedia).",
        "The throne passed to his son, Akbar, a boy of 13 or 14, who was crowned at Kalanaur, in the Punjab, on 14 February 1556. The one who ruled was the [[regent|regente]] Bairam Khan, his tutor and commander-in-chief. Bairam received the title of Khan-i-Khanan and the office of vakil, something like prime minister. The situation was fragile: the empire had barely returned, and the Afghans, the Rajputs and the rest of the north did not accept it.",
        "That was when Hemu appeared. Coming from the Rewari region, of a poor Hindu family (perhaps a saltpeter merchant, saltpeter being the raw material of gunpowder), he rose at the Sur court: he was a market inspector under Islam Shah and became prime minister and commander of King Adil Shah Suri. The sources credit him with 22 victories, almost all against Afghan rebels.",
        { h: "The road to Panipat" },
        { timeline: [
          ["January 1556", "Humayun dies. Hemu sets out from Bengal against the Mughals."],
          ["14 February 1556", "Akbar is crowned, with Bairam Khan as regent."],
          ["1556", "Hemu takes Agra and other cities."],
          ["7 October 1556", "At Tughlaqabad, near Delhi, Hemu defeats the Mughal general Tardi Beg Khan, takes Delhi and assumes the title of Vikramaditya."],
          ["October 1556", "Tardi Beg joins Bairam at Sirhind and, after a disagreement, Bairam has him executed for alleged cowardice. Akbar and Bairam march on Delhi."],
          ["5 November 1556", "The battle."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Hemu (Hemchandra Vikramaditya), about 55 years old" },
        "Born around 1501, according to Wikipedia, of humble origin and with caste and birthplace debated by historians, he was never a noble: he was an administrator who became a general, and commanded Afghan soldiers. In Delhi, he took the name Vikramaditya, a title from the ancient tradition of Indian kings. Some historians, such as Satish Chandra, doubt that he truly declared independence; others see in the title a plan to rule alone.",
        { h: "Akbar, 13 or 14 years old" },
        "Born on 15 October 1542, he was 14 on the day of the battle. Many texts say 13, the age he was when crowned, in February. He was about 13 kilometers from the field, with Bairam Khan, and did not fight. Later he would reign for nearly 50 years and become the greatest of the Mughal emperors.",
        { h: "Bairam Khan" },
        "Akbar's tutor and regent, he was the real commander of the Mughal army. He was harsh: he had Tardi Beg executed for cowardice, an accusation that Wikipedia's own text puts in doubt. In 1560, Akbar removed him from office, and Bairam was murdered on the way to Mecca, in January 1561.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Mughal side, Ali Quli Khan Shaibani commanded the center, with some 10,000 horsemen; Sikandar Khan Uzbak, the right; Abdulla Khan Uzbak, the left; and Husain Quli Beg and Shah Quli Mahram, the vanguard, which included Bairam's Turks. On Hemu's side, his nephew Ramya (or Ramaiyya, depending on the source) commanded the left wing, and Shadi Khan Kakar, the right."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to Sarkar (1960), the Mughals had about 30,000 horsemen and 200 [[war elephants|elefantes de guerra]], and Hemu some 30,000 horsemen, largely Afghans, and 500 armored elephants, with musket and crossbow shooters on their backs. Other sources speak of 1,500 elephants. Hemu had lost his artillery before the battle: Ali Quli Khan, sent ahead with about 10,000 horsemen, seized the convoy of cannons, which was poorly guarded.",
        { note: "The numbers for Panipat vary greatly, and the Mughal sources themselves, such as those of Abu'l-Fazl, have an interest in magnifying the victory. We use the ranges from modern works." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open plain, about 80 kilometers north of Delhi, at Panipat, where 30 years earlier Babur had defeated the sultan of Delhi. There was a deep ravine in front of the Mughal center, which Hemu's elephants and horses could not cross.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, Akbar and Bairam stayed some 13 kilometers behind. In front, the Mughals arranged themselves with the vanguard in front, the center behind and two wings, right and left. Hemu stayed in the center, on an elephant named Hawai.",
        "Second, Hemu opened the attack by releasing his elephants against the two Mughal wings. The soldiers who escaped the charge ran to the sides and, from there, shot arrows at Hemu's cavalry. Third, the Mughal center advanced to a defensive position in front of the ravine, and Hemu's elephants and horses, unable to cross, were left exposed to the arrows.",
        "Fourth, the Mughal cavalry went around the flanks and the rear and began to wound the elephants' legs and kill their drivers. Hemu pulled the elephants back, and the Afghan attack lost strength. Fifth, Ali Quli Khan swung his cavalry around and attacked the Afghan center from behind. Hemu reacted from his [[howdah|howdah]]: Shadi Khan Kakar and others were killed, but he kept commanding counterattacks. The fight was hard and seemed to be tilting his way.",
        "Sixth, the two Mughal wings were pushed back, and Hemu moved elephants and cavalry to crush the center. Seventh, at that moment a stray arrow struck him in the eye, and he fell unconscious. Eighth, seeing the arrow and the fallen leader, the army panicked, broke formation and fled. About 5,000 died on the field, and more in the flight. Hemu's elephant was captured a few hours later, and he was taken, still alive, to the Mughal camp."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Mughals took 120 of Hemu's elephants, and Wikipedia says this impressed them so much that elephants became part of their military strategy. Delhi and Agra were recovered. Adil Shah, the Sur king whose minister Hemu was, was defeated and killed by Khizr Khan in April 1557. Hemu's father, already old, was executed after refusing conversion, and other relatives and supporters too.",
        "Hemu was beheaded. The head was sent to Kabul, and the body was displayed in Delhi, at the Purana Quila, where he had been crowned. There is a memorial, the Hemu Samadhi Sthal, at the place of the execution, in Panipat.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The victory returned Delhi and Agra to the Mughals at a moment when the empire, with an adolescent emperor, was fragile. Akbar's reign lasted nearly 50 years. Hemu, who went from minister to king of Delhi, now has a memorial in Panipat.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Who killed Hemu? Muhammad Arif Qandhari, an author of the time, says that Akbar, advised by Bairam Khan, beheaded him to win the title of [[ghazi|ghazi]]. Abu'l-Fazl, later, says Akbar refused to strike a dying man. Modern historians tend to see the refusal as a later invention of the court. English Wikipedia leans toward Akbar, and the biography of Bairam Khan says only that it was Akbar or Bairam." },
        { note: "The numbers: the arrow in the eye and the flight are the point on which the sources agree most. The rest, including the number of elephants, changes from author to author." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  okehazama: {
    war: "Oda Nobunaga's campaigns in the Sengoku period (1560)",
    hook: "A young lord with about 3,000 men surprised the camp of a much larger army, and killed the general.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 16th-century Japan, the power of the shogun in Kyoto had come to an end in practice. Throughout the country, feudal lords, the [[daimyo|daimiô]], fought one another in an almost continuous war: this is the [[Sengoku|sengoku]] period. One of the strongest was Imagawa Yoshimoto, who controlled the provinces of Suruga, Tōtōmi and Mikawa, on the Pacific coast. Allied with the Takeda and the Hōjō, he was free to look west.",
        "In the way lay Owari, where Oda Nobunaga, son of a local chief, had just unified the province. For years, the Oda and the Imagawa had quarreled over their border. Around 1559, the Imagawa gained the castles of Narumi, Marune, Washizu and Kutsukake, which cut Owari in two, and Nobunaga responded by building forts around Narumi and Ōdaka.",
        { h: "The road to Okehazama" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1534", "Oda Nobunaga is born."],
          ["1559", "Nobunaga controls all of Owari. The Imagawa take castles on the border."],
          ["12 May 1560 (lunar calendar)", "Yoshimoto sets out from Sunpu with his army."],
          ["18 May", "Yoshimoto reaches Kutsukake."],
          ["19 May (12 June in the current calendar)", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The old idea is that Yoshimoto was marching to Kyoto to seize power from the shogun. Today, many historians reject it. Among the explanations are attacking Owari, controlling Ise Bay, relieving besieged forts and stabilizing the border."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Oda Nobunaga, 25 years old" },
        "Born on 23 June 1534, he is counted as 26 by the Japanese reckoning of the time. He was called 'the fool of Owari', for his unusual behavior: he dressed informally in public, ate melon riding backward on his horse and danced in taverns. By 1559, he had eliminated his rivals in his own family and in the province. At Okehazama, he bet everything: against a much larger enemy, only a strong offensive could make up the difference, and he ordered the counterattack.",
        { h: "Imagawa Yoshimoto, about 41 years old" },
        "Born in 1519, third son of the head of the family, he was sent to a temple as a boy, but came to lead the clan in a succession dispute in 1536. He married a sister of Takeda Shingen, defeated the Hōjō and conquered three provinces. He was the most powerful lord of the region, known as the best archer of the Tōkaidō road, an expression meaning that he was the strongest militarily there. The image of a foolish, old-fashioned noble that he has in games such as Samurai Warriors is fiction, not history.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Matsudaira Motoyasu, the future Tokugawa Ieyasu, an ally of the Imagawa, was in the vanguard and was busy supplying the fort of Ōdaka. Mōri Shinsuke and Hattori Koheita, samurai of Nobunaga, killed Yoshimoto. And Kinoshita Tōkichirō, the future Toyotomi Hideyoshi, was noticed by Nobunaga there for the first time, according to Wikipedia."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The figure most used for the Imagawa is 25,000, from the general staff of the Imperial Japanese Army, and also accepted by Kojima Tetsuo. The Shinchō kōki, the chronicle of Ōta Gyūichi, says 45,000. Other sources range between 20,000 and more than 50,000, and Yoshimoto himself announced 40,000. The important point is that only 5,000 to 6,000 of them were near Nobunaga, the rest being spread across the forts and along the road. On the Oda side, some 2,000 attacked Yoshimoto's headquarters, with 2,000 to 3,000 gathered at the fort of Zenshōji.",
        { note: "The numbers are debated. What matters most is the local difference: Nobunaga attacked at a point where he was close to the enemy in numbers, and not against the whole army." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Hills and valleys between Nagoya and the bay, in present-day Aichi. The exact place of Okehazama is still debated: a hill in Toyoake, where a park now stands, or a hill west of the fort of Nakajima. The Imagawa records say that Yoshimoto died at 'Dengaku-kubo', now identified with the Toyoake site.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "The times below come from the reconstruction in Japanese Wikipedia, based on the chronicles. First, Yoshimoto set out from Sunpu and reached Kutsukake on 18 May. Second, in the early hours of the 19th, the Imagawa attacked the forts of Marune and Washizu. Nobunaga left Kiyosu around 4 in the morning.",
        "Third, around 8, he reached the Atsuta shrine and saw the smoke from the forts. Fourth, around 10, he was at the fort of Zenshōji, where, according to tradition, he left banners to look as if he had more people. At this point, Sasa Masatsugu and Chiaki Shirō attacked the enemy vanguard with some 30 men and died.",
        "Fifth, near noon, he went to the fort of Nakajima. Yoshimoto was resting on the hill of Okehazama, in his camp, celebrating the victories, and some of his men were without armor. Sixth, around one in the afternoon, heavy rain or hail fell. The Oda advanced, the Imagawa vanguard gave way and the soldiers reached Yoshimoto's camp.",
        "Seventh, Yoshimoto came out of his tent and realized too late that those arriving were not his own. He abandoned the [[palanquin|palanquim]] and fled on horseback with some 300 guards, but was caught by repeated attacks. Eighth, according to Japanese Wikipedia, he wounded Hattori Koheita but was killed by Mōri Shinsuke. With the chief and most of the officers dead, the Imagawa surrendered or fled.",
        { h: "Attack from the front or from the rear?" },
        "The traditional version, based on Kobe's Shinchōki, says Nobunaga made a surprise attack from the side, going around the hill. Fujimoto Masayuki, based on the Shinchō kōki, argues that it was a frontal attack. Others propose a combined attack, a charge against soldiers who were looting, or an attack on an army that was already withdrawing. The idea that Yanada Masatsuna warned Nobunaga where Yoshimoto was has no support in records of the time."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The losses are uncertain. For the Oda, the Shinchō kōki speaks of about 3,000 dead, and other sources of 2,500 or 3,907. A list from the Nagafuku-ji temple gives 2,753 dead among the Imagawa and about 990 among the Oda, but researchers consider it unreliable.",
        "The Imagawa clan lost many of its main vassals and its influence in Tōtōmi and Mikawa collapsed. Motoyasu returned to Okazaki and broke with the Imagawa, allying with Nobunaga in 1562, the Kiyosu alliance. Yoshimoto was succeeded by his son Ujizane, and the clan ended up destroyed by its neighbors.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Nobunaga gained prestige and several lords swore loyalty. From 1561, he advanced on the province of Mino, took Inabayama castle in 1567, renamed it Gifu and, on 9 November 1568, entered Kyoto. It was the beginning of the unification of Japan, which his successors Hideyoshi and Ieyasu completed. Nobunaga would die in 1582, aged 47, at the Honnō-ji temple.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The battlefield was declared a National Historic Site in 1937 and is now a park in Toyoake, with the Seven Granite Pillars, the Shichikokuhyō." },
        { note: "The names: the Imagawa records say Yoshimoto died at 'Dengaku-kubo', and other texts speak of 'Dengaku-hazama'. 'Okehazama' is the name by which the battle became known." },
        { note: "The motive for the campaign: the idea that Yoshimoto was going to Kyoto appears in many books, but today it is widely contested, as seen above. What is known is that he advanced and took forts on the Owari border." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  malta: {
    war: "War between the Ottomans and the Order of St John in the Mediterranean (1565)",
    hook: "In four summer months, about 6,000 defenders withstood more than 100,000 cannon shots and an army many times larger.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Order of the Hospitallers, the knights of St John, were warrior monks who fought the Muslims in the Mediterranean. In 1522, the sultan Suleiman the Magnificent expelled them from the island of Rhodes, after a six-month siege. On 26 October 1530, they arrived in Malta, which the emperor Charles V gave them in return for one falcon a year to the viceroy of Sicily.",
        "In Malta, the knights continued to be a nuisance to the Ottomans: with galleys and corsairs, they attacked Ottoman ships. In 1551, the corsair Dragut and the admiral Sinan invaded Malta with some 10,000 men, gave up after a few days, but took the neighboring island of Gozo, where they captured about 5,000 people, and then took the knights' garrison at Tripoli. In 1552, the Order built the forts of St Michael and St Elmo, in about six months, and reinforced that of St Angelo.",
        "In mid-1564, the knight Romegas captured several cargo ships, among them one belonging to the chief of the eunuchs of the sultan's palace, and took important people prisoner. At the end of that year, Suleiman resolved to put an end to the knights of Malta.",
        { h: "The road to the siege" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1522", "Suleiman expels the Hospitallers from Rhodes."],
          ["1530", "Charles V gives Malta to the Order."],
          ["1551", "Dragut attacks Malta and Gozo and takes Tripoli."],
          ["1557", "Jean de Valette is elected grand master."],
          ["Mid-1564", "The knights capture the sultan's ships. At the end of the year, Suleiman decides to attack."],
          ["22 March 1565", "The Ottoman fleet leaves Constantinople."],
          ["18 May 1565", "The fleet reaches Malta."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Jean de Valette, about 70 years old" },
        "Elected [[grand master|grão-mestre]] in 1557, he commanded the defense. Before the Ottomans arrived, he had the crops harvested and the wells poisoned, reinforced the three forts and, on receiving a warning from a deserter, had a palisade raised at Senglea. When the knights told him that St Elmo could not hold out, he replied that the fort had to resist until reinforcements arrived. In August, he vetoed the council's proposal to withdraw to the fort of St Angelo.",
        { h: "Mustafa Pasha and Piali Pasha" },
        "Mustafa Pasha, fourth vizier, commanded the land army; Piali Pasha, the fleet. They disagreed right at the start: Piali wanted to shelter the fleet in the harbor of Marsamxett, which required taking St Elmo first; Mustafa preferred to attack Mdina and the forts by land. Mustafa gave way, thinking St Elmo would fall in a few days. It was the mistake that decided the campaign.",
        { h: "Dragut" },
        "A corsair and admiral, the most famous of the Muslims of the Mediterranean, he arrived from Tripoli in May. He was hit on the head in June, during the attack on St Elmo, and died."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Ottoman fleet had 193 ships, according to the chronicler Bosio, among them 131 [[galleys|galé]]. As for soldiers, the sources do not agree: the knight Francisco Balbi, who was a witness, says 40,000; Valette wrote 15,000 to 16,000 landed, four days after the start; Bosio, 30,000; and another author, 48,000. Wikipedia gives 35,000 to 40,000. Among them were [[Janissaries|janízaros]] and [[sipahis|sipahis]], as well as about 2,500 [[corsairs|corsário]] from Algiers.",
        "According to Balbi's table, the defenders totaled 6,100: about 500 knights, plus Spanish and Italian soldiers, 500 men from the galleys, slaves and servants, and some 3,000 Maltese. The real fighting men were about 2,500.",
        { note: "The numbers come from chroniclers who lived through the siege, and each had reasons to inflate the size of the defeated enemy. We use ranges, not a single value." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Malta is a small island. The knights held the Grand Harbour, with the fort of St Angelo at the tip of Birgu, the fort of St Michael at Senglea, and, at the harbor entrance, the small fort of St Elmo, at the tip of the Sciberras hill. Whoever took St Elmo could shelter the fleet at Marsamxett and attack the others from behind. The city of Mdina, in the center, was far from the harbor.",
        { h: "The siege, in order" },
        "First, St Elmo. The fleet arrived on 18 May, and the fighting began on the 19th. On 20 May, it anchored at Marsaxlokk, about 10 kilometers from the Grand Harbour. On 27 May, about 36 cannons began pounding the fort, which was in ruins within a week. On 3 June, the janissaries took the [[ravelin|ravelim]] and the ditch. On 16 June, there was a general assault. On 23 June, the fort fell: more than 1,500 defenders were killed, and only nine knights spared. According to the historian Sire, the fort cost the Ottomans at least 6,000 men, half of them janissaries.",
        "According to Wikipedia, when the Ottomans displayed the bodies of beheaded knights on crosses in mockery, Valette had Turkish prisoners beheaded and their heads fired from the cannons into the enemy camp. In July, about 600 soldiers, under Melchor de Robles, coming from Sicily, reached Birgu (the 'small relief').",
        "On 15 July, the attack on Senglea. Mustafa had about 100 small boats carried overland, across the Sciberras hill, to the Grand Harbour, with some 1,000 janissaries, while corsairs attacked the fort of St Michael by land. A sea-level battery that Valette had prepared sank almost all the boats, and more than 800 attackers died, killed or drowned.",
        "On 7 August, another double attack on St Michael and Birgu opened a breach in the wall, and then the Ottomans suddenly pulled back. Before that, Vincenzo Anastagi, cavalry commander at Mdina, had attacked the unprotected Ottoman field hospital and killed more than 60 men. The Turks thought the relief force had arrived.",
        "From 19 to 21 August, new attacks. According to Balbi, on 20 August Valette ran to the most threatened point and stayed there until the Turks fell back. The accounts differ: Bosio speaks of panic and friendly fire from the fort of St Angelo, and Bradford, of a mine that exploded. Balbi and Bosio do not mention the mine.",
        { h: "The end" },
        "On 7 September, a force of about 8,000 men, brought by García de Toledo, viceroy of Sicily, landed in St Paul's Bay. Mustafa prepared some 10,000 men to face them, but they were defeated near Mdina and fled to the beach, where Piali's galleys covered the embarkation. The sources give different dates for the departure: from 8 to 13 September."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Ottoman losses, according to the sources, were between 25,000 and 35,000: Bosio says 30,000, Balbi 35,000, and others 25,000. That would be about a third of the army, between those killed in combat and by disease. Of the Christians, Wikipedia gives at least 2,500 dead, but the knights lost about a third of their number, and Malta, a third of its inhabitants. Birgu and Senglea were almost razed. Balbi says some 130,000 cannon shots were fired.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The victory was celebrated across all of Europe. With the money that arrived, Valette built a new fortified city on Mount Sciberras, which was named Valletta, so that no enemy would take the position again. Six years later, the Christian alliance defeated the Ottomans at Lepanto. Some historians, such as Henry Sire, argue that the Ottomans overextended themselves and that Spain would probably have retaken the island had it fallen.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "St Elmo: Mustafa thought the fort would fall in a few days. It took almost four weeks, from 27 May to 23 June, of bombardment and assaults." },
        { note: "The death of Dragut: according to Wikipedia, a cannonball tore off a piece of a trench and struck him on the head. A rival version says it was a well-aimed shot from the fort of St Angelo." },
        { note: "The dates: English Wikipedia gives the siege as running from 18 May to 8 September, and the map's data sheet gives 11 September. The sources differ on the exact day of the Ottoman fleet's departure." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  talikota: {
    war: "War between Vijayanagara and the Deccan sultanates (1565)",
    hook: "On one day in January, the last great Hindu empire of southern India lost its regent, its army and, soon after, its capital.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Vijayanagara was founded in 1336 and, at its height, was the most powerful empire in southern India. Its capital, Vijayanagara (today Hampi), was one of the largest and richest cities in the world: around 1500, it is estimated to have been the second in the world after Beijing.",
        "To the north of the empire lay the [[Deccan|decão]], divided into Muslim sultanates. They were the heirs of the [[Bahmani Sultanate|bahmânidas]], which broke up: Ahmadnagar, Bijapur, Golconda, Bidar and Berar. The [[sultanates|sultanato]] fought one another and Vijayanagara.",
        "For more than 20 years, the regent Rama Raya took advantage of the sultanates' rivalries. He played the part of arbiter, changed allies as it suited him and advanced north of the Krishna river. According to Wikipedia, he even called himself 'Sultan of the World', which irritated his subjects as much as the sultans. Aggressive moves around Kalyan and insolent diplomacy pushed the sultans to unite, and they sealed the alliance with marriages between the royal houses.",
        { h: "The road to Talikota" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1336", "Founding of Vijayanagara."],
          ["1542", "Rama Raya takes the regency in fact, with the young Sadasiva Raya as figurehead emperor."],
          ["About 20 years", "Rama Raya plays the sultanates against one another and advances north of the Krishna river."],
          ["Early 1565", "The sultanates unite and attack, at the end of January."],
          ["23 January 1565", "The battle (Portuguese Wikipedia gives 26 January)."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Rama Raya, about 80 years old" },
        "Born in 1484, of a Telugu family from southern Andhra, he was the son-in-law of the great emperor Krishna Deva Raya. He was a skilled general, administrator and diplomat and rose under the emperor Achyuta. After 1542, the emperor was only the young Sadasiva, nearly a prisoner, and Rama Raya governed. He hired Muslim generals, and was about 80 years old in 1565, although some sources exaggerate his age. Ali Adil Shah of Bijapur was his rival, but, according to The Week magazine, Rama Raya's wife had symbolically adopted him as a son after losing her own.",
        { h: "The sultans" },
        "Four sultans led the alliance: Husain Nizam Shah, of Ahmadnagar; Ali Adil Shah, of Bijapur; Ibrahim Qutb Shah, of Golconda; and the one of Bidar. English Wikipedia says Berar did not take part; The Week includes it. Husain Nizam Shah was, according to some sources, the one who had Rama Raya killed.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Among those who fought with him were Tirumala, his brother, and Venkatadri. Two Muslim generals of the Vijayanagara army, the Gilani brothers, are the most cited in the story: according to several historians, they changed sides in the middle of the fight."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Here we have to be honest: it is not known. Some repeat 70,000 horsemen and 900,000 foot soldiers for Vijayanagara, numbers that seem exaggerated. The estimates that circulate, about 140,000 for Vijayanagara and 80,000 for the sultanates, are uncertain. The English and Portuguese Wikipedia articles give no numbers.",
        { note: "The numbers for Talikota are the least reliable in this set: the sources are chronicles written by the victors or by those who lived near them, such as Ferishta, who wrote 24 years later." },
        { h: "The weapons" },
        "Artillery weighed heavily. According to the historian Kamath, the sultanates had cannons operated by Turkish gunners, and Vijayanagara depended on European mercenaries with old cannons. Rama Raya had stopped buying Persian horses, and his cavalry used local horses. An enormous cannon, the Malik-i-Maidan, was supplied by Ali Adil Shah of Bijapur. Many scholars attribute the defeat above all to the lack of gunpowder technology on the Vijayanagara side.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The exact place is debated. The sources cite the villages of Rakkasagi and Tangadi (Rakshasi-Tangadi), near Talikota, on the banks of the Krishna river, although The Week gives the Tungabhadra river and warns that the exact site is uncertain. The duration also varies: from a few hours to a few days.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Reconstruction is very difficult, because the sources, such as the Burhan-i Maasir, Ferishta's Gulshan-i Ibrahimi, Diogo do Couto's Decades of Asia and letters from the governor of Goa, contradict one another. What can be said with some confidence is the following.",
        "Rama Raya trusted in his numbers. According to The Week, which cites the historian Manu Pillai, he moved around the field in an open litter to cheer the troops and, seated on a jewel-studded throne, handed out coins. The cannons on the Ahmadnagar side were loaded with copper coins, and the volley is said to have killed about 5,000 of Rama Raya's soldiers and landed near him. It is a chronicle figure, probably exaggerated.",
        "Then an elephant maddened by the fighting, on the Ahmadnagar side, charged toward him. According to one version, his bearers fled and he was trampled. According to another, he was captured and beheaded by Husain Nizam Shah before Ali Adil Shah arrived. His head, says Wikipedia, was stuffed with straw and displayed.",
        "For several historians (Kamath, Kulke and Rothermund), Vijayanagara was winning until the two Muslim generals, the Gilani brothers, went over to the sultanates' side. Other scholars reject this story of betrayal. When Rama Raya fell, the army fell apart. Tirumala fled with the troops, and Venkatadri was blinded and probably killed."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The sultanates' army plundered the capital, with no one to stop them. Wikipedia gives the sack as having lasted six months, with the city pillaged, looted and burned, and then abandoned. Modern historians say the destruction was concentrated in the royal center, and that the idea of total devastation is older. The ruins of Hampi are today a UNESCO World Heritage Site, since 1986.",
        "Tirumala founded the Aravidu dynasty, the last of the empire, and moved the capital to Penukonda, where it stayed from 1565 to 1592, before passing through Chandragiri and Vellore. In 1572, he abdicated and divided what was left among his three sons. The empire went on falling apart until 1646, in wars with Bijapur and others. The sultanates' alliance did not last: Bijapur was the one that gained most.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the end of the last great Hindu empire of southern India. For many scholars, the defeat had a technical cause: Vijayanagara lacked modern gunpowder. The historian Richard Eaton rejects seeing the battle as a simple clash between Hindus and Muslims, the view of colonial and nationalist historians: there were Muslims in the Vijayanagara army and rivalries among the sultans themselves. Girish Karnad's play Crossing to Talikota uses this reading.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: English Wikipedia gives 23 January 1565, and the Portuguese one gives 26 January." },
        { note: "The Gilani betrayal is the best-known explanation, but the sources that tell it are late, and some scholars link it to an account by the Venetian merchant Cesare Federici, written afterward." },
        { note: "Rama Raya's end has two versions (trampled or beheaded in captivity) and neither can be proven." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

